# Salesforce Apex & LWC — Clean Code Rules

## General Principles
- Follow Clean Code principles (Robert C. Martin): readable, maintainable, and self-documenting code
- Favor clarity over cleverness — code is read far more than it is written
- One responsibility per class, method, or component
- Avoid duplication (DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself)
- Leave code cleaner than you found it (Boy Scout Rule)

---

## Apex — Naming Conventions
- Classes: `PascalCase`, noun-based, descriptive (e.g., `OpportunityService`, `AccountRepository`)
- Methods: `camelCase`, verb-based (e.g., `getActiveAccounts()`, `calculateDiscount()`)
- Variables: `camelCase`, meaningful names — never `x`, `temp`, `data`, `obj`
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` (e.g., `MAX_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`)
- Test classes: suffix with `Test` (e.g., `OpportunityServiceTest`)
- No abbreviations unless universally understood (e.g., `Id`, `URL` are fine)

## Apex — Class Design
- One class = one responsibility (Single Responsibility Principle)
- Keep classes under 300 lines; extract logic into helper/service classes if larger
- Use `private` visibility by default; only expose what is needed
- Avoid `static` utility god-classes — use proper service/domain layering:
  - **Controller**: thin, handles UI/API requests only
  - **Service**: business logic
  - **Selector/Repository**: SOQL queries only
  - **Domain**: record-level logic (trigger handlers, validation)
- Use `with sharing` unless there is a documented reason not to
- Prefer dependency injection over hardcoded dependencies for testability

## Apex — Methods
- Methods should do ONE thing and do it well
- Keep methods under 20 lines; extract sub-steps into named helper methods
- Method names should describe WHAT they do, not HOW (e.g., `getExpiredContracts()` not `queryContractsByEndDate()`)
- Avoid boolean flag parameters — split into two methods instead
- Return early to reduce nesting (guard clauses)
- No dead code — remove unused methods and variables

## Apex — SOQL & DML
- Never put SOQL or DML inside loops — always bulkify
- Use selector classes to centralize all queries
- Always query only the fields you need
- Use `FOR UPDATE` explicitly when locking is required
- Wrap DML in try/catch only when you handle the exception meaningfully
- Use `Database.insert(records, false)` with error handling for partial saves when appropriate

## Apex — Error Handling
- Never swallow exceptions silently
- Log errors using a consistent logging framework (e.g., `Logger.error()`)
- Throw meaningful custom exceptions with descriptive messages
- Validate inputs at service boundaries, not deep in helper methods

## Apex — Testing
- Every class must have a corresponding test class with 85%+ coverage minimum (aim for 100%)
- Tests must assert behavior, not just execute lines
- Use `@TestSetup` for shared test data
- Use descriptive test method names: `test_getActiveAccounts_returnsOnlyActiveRecords()`
- Never use `seeAllData=true`
- Use `Test.startTest()` / `Test.stopTest()` to isolate governor limits
- Mock external callouts using `HttpCalloutMock`
- One logical assertion per test where possible

## Apex — Comments & Documentation
- Code should be self-documenting — use comments only to explain WHY, never WHAT
- Add ApexDoc headers to all `public` and `global` methods
- Remove all commented-out code before committing
- TODO comments must include a ticket/issue reference

