## LWC — Naming Conventions
- Component folders: `camelCase` (e.g., `accountSummaryCard`, `opportunityList`)
- JavaScript variables/methods: `camelCase`
- CSS classes: `kebab-case` (e.g., `card-header`, `action-button`)
- Wire properties: prefix with `$` only when passing as reactive property (Salesforce standard)
- Event names: `kebab-case`, past-tense (e.g., `record-selected`, `form-submitted`)
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` in a shared `constants.js` if reused across components

## LWC — Component Design
- One component = one responsibility (avoid multi-purpose "mega components")
- Split large templates into child components — templates over 100 lines are a smell
- Smart vs. Dumb component pattern:
  - **Smart (container)**: fetches data, manages state, handles logic
  - **Dumb (presentational)**: receives data via `@api`, fires events upward
- Avoid deep nesting of conditional rendering — extract into sub-components
- Keep `@api` properties minimal and well-typed with JSDoc

## LWC — JavaScript
- Keep JS files under 150 lines; extract utilities into `utils.js` or shared modules
- No business logic in templates — move to JS or service layers
- Use `@wire` for read data when possible; use imperative Apex only when you need control flow
- Always handle wire/imperative errors (e.g., with `reduceErrors`)
- Use `async/await` over `.then()` chains for readability
- Avoid `console.log` in production code
- Clean up event listeners and subscriptions in `disconnectedCallback`
- Use ES6+ features: destructuring, spread, optional chaining, nullish coalescing

## LWC — Templates (HTML)
- Keep templates readable — one concept per section, logical top-to-bottom flow
- Use meaningful `if:true` / `iterator` variable names
- Always add a `key` to `for:each` iterations using a unique record field (e.g., `key={item.Id}`)
- Use `lwc:if`, `lwc:elseif`, `lwc:else` (API v55+) instead of legacy `if:true`
- Avoid inline styles — use CSS classes
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible (use `<button>`, not `<div onclick>`)
- Use ARIA attributes for custom interactive elements

## LWC — CSS
- Scope styles to the component — avoid `:host` overrides of Lightning Base Components unless necessary
- Use CSS custom properties (Design Tokens) for colors, spacing, and typography
- No `!important` unless absolutely unavoidable (document why)
- Mobile-first responsive design using `@media` queries

## LWC — Events & Communication
- Parent → Child: use `@api` properties
- Child → Parent: use custom events with `bubbles: false, composed: false` by default
- Sibling communication: use a shared parent or Lightning Message Service (LMS)
- Publish/Subscribe (PubSub) is discouraged — prefer LMS for cross-component communication
- Always document custom events with JSDoc

## LWC — Testing
- Write Jest tests for all non-trivial components
- Test user interactions, not implementation details
- Mock Apex calls and wire adapters using `@salesforce/apex` mocks
- Test error states explicitly
- Use `querySelector` with accessible selectors when possible

---

## File & Folder Structure
```
force-app/main/default/
  classes/
    AccountService.cls          # Business logic
    AccountServiceTest.cls      # Corresponding test
    AccountSelector.cls         # SOQL queries only
    AccountDomain.cls           # Trigger/record logic
  lwc/
    accountSummaryCard/         # Smart container
    accountSummaryCardItem/     # Dumb child component
    sharedUtils/                # Shared JS utilities
```

---

## Commit & PR Standards
- Commits must reference a ticket: `feat(JIRA-456): add bulk account reassignment logic`
- No commented-out code in PRs
- No `System.debug` statements left in production Apex
- All new Apex must have test coverage verified before merge
- Follow the Conventional Commits format: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`
